  "title": "Erdős Problem #912",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "If $ n! = \\prod_i p_i^{k_i} $ is the factorisation into distinct primes then let $h(n)$ count the number of distinct exponents $k_i$.\nProve that there exists some $c>0$ such that $ h(n) \\sim c \\left(\\frac{n}{\\log n}\\right)^{1/2} $ as $n\\to \\infty$.",
